const a=String.raw,t=[{id:"early",title:"Early roots",span:"before 1750",blurb:"Statecraft, trade and taxation were analysed long before economics had a name."},{id:"classical",title:"Classical economics",span:"1750 to 1870",blurb:"Markets, the division of labour, trade, population and distribution between classes."},{id:"neoclassical",title:"The marginal revolution",span:"1870 to 1930",blurb:"Marginal thinking, supply and demand, equilibrium and welfare."},{id:"keynes",title:"Keynes and the mid-century synthesis",span:"1930 to 1970",blurb:"Depression, aggregate demand, growth, mathematical economics and national accounts."},{id:"modern",title:"Expectations, information and institutions",span:"1970 to 2000",blurb:"Rational expectations, information problems, game theory, institutions and new growth theory."},{id:"evidence",title:"Behaviour, evidence and design",span:"2000 to today",blurb:"Psychology, experiments, natural experiments, market design, inequality and innovation."}],e=i=>i,n=[e({id:"kautilya",name:"Kautilya (Chanakya)",years:"Traditionally 4th century BCE",from:"Ancient India",era:"early",school:"Statecraft",idea:"A treatise on running a state's economy: taxation, trade, prices, labour and public finance.",explain:"The Arthashastra, traditionally attributed to Kautilya, adviser to Chandragupta Maurya, discusses how a king should raise revenue without destroying the tax base, regulate markets and weights, manage state enterprises and protect against famine. Scholars date the text's compilation anywhere between about the 4th century BCE and the 3rd century CE, so treat attributions with care.",works:["Arthashastra"],influence:"Shows that ideas such as moderate taxation, price regulation and public granaries have deep South Asian roots.",critique:"Written for a ruler, not as a theory of markets; much is administrative rules rather than analysis.",today:"A good starting point if you want to write about the history of economic thought in South Asia, a small but respected niche.",specs:["thought"]}),e({id:"ibn-khaldun",name:"Ibn Khaldun",years:"1332 to 1406",from:"North Africa",era:"early",school:"Historical sociology",idea:"High tax rates can shrink the tax base and with it the state's revenue.",explain:"In the Muqaddimah (1377), Ibn Khaldun described how dynasties rise and fall. Early on, taxes are low and enterprise flourishes; later, rulers raise rates, people produce less, and revenue falls. The same logic later appears in the Laffer curve. He also wrote about the division of labour and about how cities grow.",works:["Muqaddimah (1377)"],influence:"Often cited as a forerunner of public finance, growth and institutional economics.",critique:"A historical narrative, not a formal theory; modern readers can read too much into it.",today:"The idea that the tax base responds to rates is central to optimal tax theory and to debates about revenue in Nepal.",topics:[],specs:["public-finance","thought"]}),e({id:"smith",name:"Adam Smith",years:"1723 to 1790",from:"Scotland",era:"classical",school:"Classical",idea:"Self-interest, guided by competition, can produce social benefit: the invisible hand. Wealth comes from productivity, and productivity from the division of labour.",explain:"Smith's pin factory shows how splitting work into small tasks multiplies output. The size of the market limits how far the division of labour can go, which is why trade and good roads matter. Competition turns private self-interest into lower prices and better goods. Smith was not a simple defender of business: he warned that merchants conspire to raise prices and argued for public education and public works. His earlier book on moral sentiments stressed sympathy and social norms.",works:["The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759)","The Wealth of Nations (1776)"],influence:"Founded economics as a discipline: markets, specialisation, trade and the role of the state.",critique:"The invisible hand fails with externalities, market power and information problems, which Smith partly recognised and later economists formalised.",today:"For Nepal, the link between market size, transport costs and specialisation is still the key growth question for a landlocked mountain economy.",specs:["thought","growth"]}),e({id:"malthus",name:"Thomas Robert Malthus",years:"1766 to 1834",from:"England",era:"classical",school:"Classical",idea:"Population tends to grow faster than food supply, keeping most people near subsistence.",explain:"If income rises above subsistence, families have more children; with land fixed, more people means less food each, and income falls back. This Malthusian trap describes most of human history fairly well. It broke down after the Industrial Revolution because technology grew faster than population and families chose fewer children as incomes rose.",model:a`y_t = A\left(\frac{X}{L_t}\right)^{\beta}, \quad L_{t+1} = L_t\, n(y_t)`,works:["An Essay on the Principle of Population (1798)"],influence:"Unified growth theory (Galor) and economic history still use Malthusian dynamics to explain the pre-industrial world.",critique:"Failed as a forecast for modern economies; underestimated technology and the demographic transition.",today:"Nepal's fertility has fallen sharply (to around two children per woman), a demographic transition that shapes labour supply, migration and pensions.",specs:["demography","econ-history"]}),e({id:"ricardo",name:"David Ricardo",years:"1772 to 1823",from:"England",era:"classical",school:"Classical",idea:"Comparative advantage: countries gain from trade even if one is better at producing everything.",explain:"What matters is opportunity cost. If Portugal is better at making both wine and cloth but relatively much better at wine, both countries gain when Portugal makes wine and England makes cloth and they trade. Ricardo also developed the theory of land rent (rent comes from differences in land quality) and the idea, later called Ricardian equivalence, that borrowing and taxing can have the same effect.",model:a`\frac{a_{L,wine}^{P}}{a_{L,cloth}^{P}} < \frac{a_{L,wine}^{E}}{a_{L,cloth}^{E}} \;\Rightarrow\; \text{Portugal exports wine}`,works:["On the Principles of Political Economy and Taxation (1817)"],influence:"Comparative advantage is the foundation of trade theory; modern Ricardian models (Eaton and Kortum) are used in quantitative trade research.",critique:"Ignores adjustment costs and the losers from trade within countries; assumes full employment.",today:"Nepal's comparative advantage in hydropower, tourism and high value crops is a live policy debate.",topics:[],specs:["trade"]}),e({id:"mill",name:"John Stuart Mill",years:"1806 to 1873",from:"England",era:"classical",school:"Classical and utilitarian",idea:"Production follows natural laws, but distribution is a matter of social choice.",explain:"Mill wrote the leading economics textbook of the 19th century. He separated the laws of production, which he saw as fixed by technology, from the distribution of income, which societies can change through institutions and taxation. He developed reciprocal demand in trade theory, defended liberty and argued for women's rights and education.",works:["Principles of Political Economy (1848)","On Liberty (1859)","The Subjection of Women (1869)"],influence:"Shaped welfare economics and liberal political economy.",critique:"Some ideas (the wages fund) were later abandoned.",today:"The separation of efficiency and distribution is the logic of the second welfare theorem and of redistributive policy.",specs:["thought","inequality"]}),e({id:"marx",name:"Karl Marx",years:"1818 to 1883",from:"Germany",era:"classical",school:"Marxian",idea:"Capitalism is driven by capital accumulation and class conflict; profits come from the unpaid labour of workers.",explain:"Marx used a labour theory of value: the value of goods comes from the labour time needed to make them. Workers are paid the value of their labour power but produce more, and the difference (surplus value) becomes profit. Competition forces capitalists to accumulate and mechanise, which Marx expected to cause crises and falling profit rates. He also analysed technological change, concentration of industry and business cycles.",works:["The Communist Manifesto (1848, with Engels)","Capital, volume 1 (1867)"],influence:"Enormous political influence; in economics, his questions about distribution, technology and crises remain relevant even where his answers are rejected.",critique:"The labour theory of value is rejected by mainstream economics; predictions of immiseration and collapse did not hold in market economies.",today:"Nepal's politics have strong Marxist-Leninist and Maoist parties, so understanding Marx helps you understand Nepali policy debates about land, labour and the state.",specs:["thought","transition"]}),e({id:"walras",name:"Léon Walras",years:"1834 to 1910",from:"France and Switzerland",era:"neoclassical",school:"Neoclassical (Lausanne)",idea:"All markets are connected: prices are determined together in a general equilibrium.",explain:"Walras wrote down a system of equations for all markets at once and asked whether a set of prices exists that clears them all. He imagined an auctioneer adjusting prices until excess demand disappears (tatonnement). Walras' law says that if all but one market clear, the last must clear too.",model:a`\sum_i p_i\, z_i(p) = 0 \quad \text{(Walras' law)}`,works:["Elements of Pure Economics (1874)"],influence:"Founded general equilibrium theory, completed by Arrow and Debreu in 1954.",critique:"The auctioneer is a fiction; real price adjustment is decentralised and slow.",today:"Computable general equilibrium models used for trade and tax policy (including for Nepal) are descendants of Walras.",specs:["micro-theory"]}),e({id:"marshall",name:"Alfred Marshall",years:"1842 to 1924",from:"England",era:"neoclassical",school:"Neoclassical (Cambridge)",idea:"Supply and demand as two blades of a pair of scissors; partial equilibrium, elasticity and consumer surplus.",explain:"Marshall's Principles made economics a practical science. He analysed one market at a time holding others fixed (ceteris paribus), introduced price elasticity, consumer and producer surplus, the short run and the long run, and external economies from industrial districts where firms learn from each other. He drew price on the vertical axis, a convention still used.",works:["Principles of Economics (1890)"],influence:"Every introductory course is Marshallian. Agglomeration economics starts from his industrial districts.",critique:"Partial equilibrium misses feedback across markets.",today:"Marshallian externalities explain why Kathmandu valley attracts firms despite congestion.",topics:["welfare-measures"],specs:["urban","io"]}),e({id:"pareto",name:"Vilfredo Pareto",years:"1848 to 1923",from:"Italy",era:"neoclassical",school:"Neoclassical (Lausanne)",idea:"Pareto efficiency: an allocation where no one can be made better off without making someone worse off.",explain:"Pareto replaced measurable utility with ordinal preferences (rankings) and defined efficiency without comparing people's happiness. He also found that the top of income distributions follows a power law, now called the Pareto distribution.",model:a`P(Y > y) = \left(\frac{y_m}{y}\right)^{\alpha}`,works:["Manual of Political Economy (1906)"],influence:"Pareto efficiency is the basic welfare criterion; the Pareto distribution is used to estimate top incomes.",critique:"Efficiency says nothing about fairness: an allocation where one person has everything can be Pareto efficient.",today:"Top income research (Piketty, Saez) fits Pareto tails to tax data.",specs:["micro-theory","inequality"]}),e({id:"fisher",name:"Irving Fisher",years:"1867 to 1947",from:"United States",era:"neoclassical",school:"Neoclassical",idea:"The real interest rate links saving and investment over time; inflation shifts nominal rates; debt deflation deepens depressions.",explain:"Fisher modelled saving as a choice between consumption now and later, giving the Fisher equation linking nominal and real interest. He wrote the equation of exchange MV = PT, built early price indexes, and after losing his fortune in 1929 explained how falling prices raise the real burden of debt, causing more defaults and further price falls.",model:a`1 + i = (1 + r)(1 + \pi^e)`,works:["The Purchasing Power of Money (1911)","The Theory of Interest (1930)","The Debt-Deflation Theory of Great Depressions (1933)"],influence:"Intertemporal choice, the Fisher equation and index number theory are standard.",critique:"Famously predicted in 1929 that stock prices had reached a permanently high plateau.",today:"Real interest rates on Nepali deposits, and debt deflation risks when land prices fall, both use Fisher's logic.",specs:["monetary"]}),e({id:"pigou",name:"Arthur Cecil Pigou",years:"1877 to 1959",from:"England",era:"neoclassical",school:"Neoclassical (Cambridge)",idea:"When private and social costs differ, taxes or subsidies can correct the market.",explain:"A factory that pollutes imposes costs on others that it does not pay. Pigou proposed taxing the activity by the amount of the external damage so that private decisions reflect social costs. He founded welfare economics as a field.",model:a`t^* = MSC - MPC = MED`,works:["The Economics of Welfare (1920)"],influence:"Carbon taxes, congestion charges and tobacco taxes are Pigouvian.",critique:"Coase showed bargaining can solve some externalities; measuring damage is hard.",today:"Brick kiln pollution in the Kathmandu valley is a textbook Pigouvian problem.",specs:["environment","public-finance"]}),e({id:"keynes",name:"John Maynard Keynes",years:"1883 to 1946",from:"England",era:"keynes",school:"Keynesian",idea:"Output and employment are set by aggregate demand in the short run; economies can stay stuck in unemployment without policy.",explain:"In the Great Depression, wages and prices fell but unemployment stayed high. Keynes argued that saving and investment are done by different people for different reasons, so planned spending can fall short of output. Firms then cut production, incomes fall and spending falls again: the multiplier works in reverse. Uncertainty and 'animal spirits' drive investment, and in a liquidity trap monetary policy loses power, so government spending must fill the gap. He also helped design the Bretton Woods system, creating the IMF and World Bank.",model:a`Y = C(Y - T) + I(r) + G, \qquad k = \frac{1}{1 - c}`,works:["The Economic Consequences of the Peace (1919)","The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936)"],influence:"Created macroeconomics as a field. Fiscal stimulus in 2008 and 2020 followed Keynesian logic.",critique:"Monetarists and new classical economists criticised the neglect of expectations and the long run; persistent deficits can be costly.",today:"For a small open economy like Nepal, imports leak demand abroad, so multipliers are small; that refinement of Keynes is a good thesis theme.",topics:["is-lm-ad-as"],specs:["macro-theory","public-finance"]}),e({id:"schumpeter",name:"Joseph Schumpeter",years:"1883 to 1950",from:"Austria and United States",era:"keynes",school:"Austrian and evolutionary",idea:"Creative destruction: growth comes from entrepreneurs whose innovations destroy old firms and industries.",explain:"Schumpeter saw capitalism as a process of change, not a static equilibrium. Entrepreneurs introduce new products and methods, earn temporary monopoly profits, and are copied or overtaken. Old firms die, new ones grow. He worried that large bureaucratic corporations might eventually replace entrepreneurs.",works:["The Theory of Economic Development (1911)","Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy (1942)"],influence:"Inspired endogenous growth theory, especially Aghion and Howitt, who shared the 2025 Nobel prize partly for formalising creative destruction.",critique:"Hard to test without modern firm data, which is why Schumpeterian ideas became empirical only recently.",today:"Firm entry, exit and growth in Nepal's manufacturing census data can test whether creative destruction operates.",specs:["innovation","growth"]}),e({id:"hicks",name:"John Hicks",years:"1904 to 1989",from:"England",era:"keynes",school:"Neoclassical synthesis",nobel:"1972 (with Kenneth Arrow)",idea:"The IS-LM model, and the split of price effects into substitution and income effects.",explain:"In 1937 Hicks summarised Keynes in two curves: IS (goods market) and LM (money market). Their intersection gives output and the interest rate. In consumer theory he introduced compensated (Hicksian) demand, which separates substitution from income effects.",model:a`IS: Y = C(Y) + I(r) + G, \qquad LM: \frac{M}{P} = L(Y, r)`,works:["Mr. Keynes and the Classics (1937)","Value and Capital (1939)"],influence:"IS-LM structured macro teaching for half a century; Hicksian demand is basic consumer theory.",critique:"Hicks himself later said IS-LM oversimplified Keynes, especially uncertainty.",today:"IS-LM with an exchange rate peg is still a good first model of Nepal's macroeconomy.",topics:["is-lm-ad-as","duality-slutsky"],specs:["macro-theory","micro-theory"]}),e({id:"hayek",name:"Friedrich Hayek",years:"1899 to 1992",from:"Austria and England",era:"keynes",school:"Austrian",nobel:"1974 (with Gunnar Myrdal)",idea:"Prices transmit dispersed knowledge that no central planner could collect.",explain:"Information about local conditions is spread among millions of people. A price change tells everyone that something has become scarce without telling them why, and they adjust. Hayek argued that central planning fails because it cannot use this knowledge, and he debated Keynes on business cycles.",works:["The Road to Serfdom (1944)","The Use of Knowledge in Society (1945)"],influence:"Information economics and mechanism design formalised his insight about dispersed knowledge.",critique:"Markets also fail through externalities and information asymmetries; Austrian business cycle theory lacks empirical support.",today:"Debates about price controls and state enterprises in Nepal echo Hayek's argument.",specs:["thought","information-contracts"]}),e({id:"myrdal",name:"Gunnar Myrdal",years:"1898 to 1987",from:"Sweden",era:"keynes",school:"Institutional",nobel:"1974 (with Friedrich Hayek)",idea:"Poverty and inequality reinforce themselves through cumulative causation; institutions and attitudes matter for development.",explain:"Myrdal argued that once a region falls behind, capital and skilled people leave, making it fall further behind. His study of South Asia, Asian Drama, examined why development was slow and stressed institutions, corruption (the 'soft state') and social structure.",works:["An American Dilemma (1944)","Asian Drama: An Inquiry into the Poverty of Nations (1968)"],influence:"An early institutional view of development; inspired regional economics.",critique:"Often pessimistic; East Asia's rapid growth contradicted some predictions.",today:"Asian Drama is about South Asia; its themes of weak state capacity remain relevant for Nepal.",specs:["development","institutions"]}),e({id:"robinson-joan",name:"Joan Robinson",years:"1903 to 1983",from:"England",era:"keynes",school:"Post-Keynesian",idea:"Imperfect competition and monopsony: employers with wage-setting power pay workers less than their marginal product.",explain:"Robinson analysed markets between perfect competition and monopoly and coined the term monopsony for a single buyer. A monopsony employer hires fewer workers at a lower wage than in a competitive market. She later criticised neoclassical capital theory in the Cambridge capital controversy.",works:["The Economics of Imperfect Competition (1933)"],influence:"Monopsony is now central to labour economics and minimum wage debates.",critique:"Her later work was outside the mainstream.",today:"Labour markets in small towns dominated by one employer, such as a tea estate, fit the monopsony model.",specs:["labour","io"]}),e({id:"samuelson",name:"Paul Samuelson",years:"1915 to 2009",from:"United States",era:"keynes",school:"Neoclassical synthesis",nobel:"1970",idea:"Economics as constrained optimisation and equilibrium, written in mathematics.",explain:"Samuelson showed that most of economics follows from maximisation under constraints and the stability of equilibrium. He developed revealed preference, the theory of public goods, the Stolper-Samuelson theorem on trade and wages, and wrote the best-selling textbook that taught generations.",model:a`\sum_i MRS^i_{G,x} = MRT_{G,x} \quad \text{(Samuelson condition for public goods)}`,works:["Foundations of Economic Analysis (1947)","Economics (textbook, 1948)","The Pure Theory of Public Expenditure (1954)"],influence:"Made mathematics the language of economics.",critique:"Critics say the mathematical turn sometimes valued elegance over realism.",today:"Public goods theory guides whether Nepal's local governments or the centre should provide services.",specs:["micro-theory","public-finance"]}),e({id:"arrow",name:"Kenneth Arrow",years:"1921 to 2017",from:"United States",era:"keynes",school:"Neoclassical",nobel:"1972 (with John Hicks)",idea:"General equilibrium exists and is efficient under conditions; no voting rule satisfies all reasonable fairness conditions.",explain:"With Debreu, Arrow proved that a competitive equilibrium exists in an economy with many markets. His impossibility theorem shows no method of combining individual rankings into a social ranking satisfies a short list of reasonable conditions. His 1963 paper on medical care founded health economics and the study of uncertainty and information.",works:["Social Choice and Individual Values (1951)","Existence of an Equilibrium for a Competitive Economy (1954, with Debreu)","Uncertainty and the Welfare Economics of Medical Care (1963)"],influence:"Foundations of general equilibrium, social choice and health economics.",critique:"The assumptions for existence and efficiency (complete markets) rarely hold.",today:"Health insurance design in Nepal faces exactly the problems Arrow described.",specs:["micro-theory","health"]}),e({id:"kuznets",name:"Simon Kuznets",years:"1901 to 1985",from:"Belarus and United States",era:"keynes",school:"Empirical",nobel:"1971",idea:"Measure the economy: national income accounts; inequality may first rise then fall with development.",explain:"Kuznets built the first systematic estimates of national income, which became GDP. He warned that GDP does not measure welfare. In 1955 he suggested that inequality rises in early industrialisation and falls later, the Kuznets curve.",works:["National Income and Its Composition (1941)","Economic Growth and Income Inequality (1955)"],influence:"Every national accounts system descends from his work.",critique:"The Kuznets curve is not a reliable law; inequality rose again in rich countries after 1980.",today:"Nepal's National Statistics Office compiles GDP using these concepts; knowing their limits matters for research.",topics:["national-accounts"],specs:["growth","inequality"]}),e({id:"friedman",name:"Milton Friedman",years:"1912 to 2006",from:"United States",era:"keynes",school:"Monetarist (Chicago)",nobel:"1976",idea:"Money matters: inflation is always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon; consumption depends on permanent income; there is no lasting inflation-unemployment trade-off.",explain:"With Anna Schwartz, Friedman argued the Great Depression was deepened by the Fed letting the money supply collapse. His permanent income hypothesis says people smooth consumption based on long run income. In 1968 he argued, with Phelps, that unemployment returns to a natural rate whatever the inflation rate, predicting stagflation before it happened.",model:a`\pi_t = \pi^e_t - \alpha(u_t - u^*)`,works:["A Theory of the Consumption Function (1957)","A Monetary History of the United States (1963, with Anna Schwartz)","The Role of Monetary Policy (1968)"],influence:"Inflation targeting and central bank independence owe much to monetarism.",critique:"Money growth targets failed in practice in the 1980s as velocity became unstable.",today:"Remittance windfalls in Nepal are a natural test of the permanent income hypothesis: are they spent or saved?",topics:["consumption","phillips-curve"],specs:["monetary","macro-theory"]}),e({id:"solow",name:"Robert Solow",years:"1924 to 2023",from:"United States",era:"keynes",school:"Neoclassical",nobel:"1987",idea:"Capital accumulation cannot sustain growth forever; long run growth comes from technology.",explain:"In Solow's model, diminishing returns mean more capital raises output by less and less. Saving changes the level of income, not long run growth. When he measured US growth, most of it came not from capital or labour but from a residual he attributed to technical progress.",model:a`\dot k = s f(k) - (n + \delta) k`,works:["A Contribution to the Theory of Economic Growth (1956)","Technical Change and the Aggregate Production Function (1957)"],influence:"The starting point of all growth economics and growth accounting.",critique:"Technology is unexplained (exogenous), which endogenous growth theory addressed.",today:"Solow's framework is the first tool to analyse why Nepal's investment has not produced faster growth.",topics:["solow"],specs:["growth"]}),e({id:"lewis",name:"W. Arthur Lewis",years:"1915 to 1991",from:"Saint Lucia",era:"keynes",school:"Development (structuralist)",nobel:"1979 (with Theodore Schultz)",idea:"Development means moving surplus labour from low productivity traditional sectors to high productivity modern sectors.",explain:"In Lewis's dual economy, agriculture has many workers whose marginal product is close to zero. Industry can hire them at a constant wage, and profits are reinvested, so industry grows until surplus labour runs out (the Lewis turning point), after which wages rise.",model:a`w_{modern} = \bar w \text{ until surplus labour is absorbed}`,works:["Economic Development with Unlimited Supplies of Labour (1954)"],influence:"Structural transformation remains central to development economics.",critique:"Agricultural labour is rarely completely surplus; urban unemployment (Harris-Todaro) complicates the picture.",today:"In Nepal, surplus rural labour has moved abroad rather than into domestic industry: a twist on Lewis worth researching.",specs:["development","migration"]}),e({id:"prebisch",name:"Raúl Prebisch",years:"1901 to 1986",from:"Argentina",era:"keynes",school:"Structuralist",idea:"The terms of trade of commodity exporters tend to decline relative to manufactures.",explain:"Prebisch, and independently Hans Singer, argued that prices of primary commodities fall over time relative to manufactured goods, so countries specialising in raw materials fall behind. This motivated import substitution industrialisation in Latin America and elsewhere.",works:["The Economic Development of Latin America and its Principal Problems (1950)"],influence:"Shaped development policy and UNCTAD, which he led.",critique:"Evidence on declining terms of trade is mixed; import substitution often produced inefficient industries.",today:"Nepal's import substitution policies before the 1990s liberalisation are a case study.",specs:["development","trade"]}),e({id:"tinbergen",name:"Jan Tinbergen",years:"1903 to 1994",from:"Netherlands",era:"keynes",school:"Econometrics and planning",nobel:"1969 (with Ragnar Frisch)",idea:"You need as many policy instruments as policy targets; the first macroeconometric models; the gravity model of trade.",explain:"Tinbergen's rule says one instrument cannot hit two independent goals. He built the first economy-wide statistical models and, in 1962, proposed that trade between two countries rises with their sizes and falls with distance: the gravity equation.",model:a`X_{ij} = G\,\frac{Y_i^{\alpha} Y_j^{\beta}}{D_{ij}^{\theta}}`,works:["Shaping the World Economy (1962)","On the Theory of Economic Policy (1952)"],influence:"The gravity model is the workhorse of empirical trade.",critique:"Early models lacked microfoundations (later supplied by Anderson and others).",today:"A gravity model of Nepal's trade shows how much distance and India's transit matter.",specs:["trade","applied-econometrics"]}),e({id:"nash",name:"John Nash",years:"1928 to 2015",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"Game theory",nobel:"1994 (with John Harsanyi and Reinhard Selten)",idea:"Nash equilibrium: a set of strategies where no player can gain by changing strategy alone.",explain:"Nash proved that every finite game has at least one equilibrium if players can randomise. This made game theory usable across economics, from oligopoly to bargaining. Harsanyi extended it to incomplete information and Selten to dynamic games (subgame perfection).",model:a`u_i(s_i^*, s_{-i}^*) \ge u_i(s_i, s_{-i}^*) \;\; \forall s_i, \forall i`,works:["Equilibrium Points in n-Person Games (1950)","Non-Cooperative Games (1951)","The Bargaining Problem (1950)"],influence:"Game theory is now the language of IO, contract theory, political economy and market design.",critique:"Many games have several equilibria; which one occurs needs extra theory.",today:"Coalition bargaining in Nepal's parliament and cooperation in community forest groups are strategic games.",topics:["game-theory-nash"],specs:["game-theory"]}),e({id:"becker",name:"Gary Becker",years:"1930 to 2014",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"Chicago",nobel:"1992",idea:"Economic reasoning applies to all human behaviour: education, family, crime and discrimination.",explain:"Becker treated education as an investment in human capital, with returns like any other investment. He modelled discrimination as a taste that costs discriminating employers profit, crime as a choice responding to penalties and probabilities, and the family as a small economy.",model:a`\text{invest in schooling if } \sum_t \frac{\Delta w_t}{(1 + r)^t} > \text{cost}`,works:["The Economics of Discrimination (1957)","Human Capital (1964)","A Treatise on the Family (1981)"],influence:"Human capital is central to labour, development and growth economics.",critique:"Critics say it overextends rational choice; behavioural economics challenges some assumptions.",today:"Returns to education in Nepal, and the choice between schooling and migration, are Beckerian questions.",specs:["education","labour"]}),e({id:"coase",name:"Ronald Coase",years:"1910 to 2013",from:"England and United States",era:"modern",school:"New institutional",nobel:"1991",idea:"Firms exist because markets have transaction costs; with low transaction costs, bargaining can solve externalities whoever holds the rights.",explain:"Why are some activities organised inside firms rather than through markets? Because using markets has costs: finding partners, negotiating, enforcing. In 1960 he showed that if property rights are clear and bargaining is cheap, people negotiate to the efficient outcome regardless of who has the rights. Since bargaining is often costly, institutions and the assignment of rights matter.",works:["The Nature of the Firm (1937)","The Problem of Social Cost (1960)"],influence:"Founded transaction cost economics and law and economics.",critique:"The Coase theorem's conditions are rarely met with many parties.",today:"Disputes over water rights between upstream and downstream villages in Nepal are Coasean bargaining problems.",specs:["law-econ","institutions","io"]}),e({id:"lucas",name:"Robert Lucas",years:"1937 to 2023",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"New classical",nobel:"1995",idea:"Rational expectations and the Lucas critique: policy changes alter behaviour, so models must be built on stable preferences and technology.",explain:"If people form expectations using all available information, they anticipate systematic policy and adjust. A model estimated under one policy can then give wrong answers under another, because the estimated relationships themselves change. This pushed macroeconomics toward models built from optimising agents (microfoundations). Lucas also wrote influential work on human capital and growth.",works:["Expectations and the Neutrality of Money (1972)","Econometric Policy Evaluation: A Critique (1976)","On the Mechanics of Economic Development (1988)"],influence:"DSGE models used by central banks follow from his program.",critique:"Fully rational expectations are strong; the 2008 crisis exposed limits of models without financial frictions.",today:"Any study of how a new Nepali policy changes behaviour should consider the Lucas critique.",specs:["macro-theory","growth"]}),e({id:"kydland-prescott",name:"Finn Kydland and Edward Prescott",years:"born 1943; 1940 to 2022",from:"Norway and United States",era:"modern",school:"New classical (real business cycles)",nobel:"2004",idea:"Time inconsistency: policymakers who can reoptimise each period produce worse outcomes than those bound by rules. Business cycles can come from productivity shocks.",explain:"A government that promises low inflation is tempted later to inflate to cut unemployment; people anticipate this, so inflation ends up high with no gain. Credible rules or independent central banks solve this. Their real business cycle model showed that a calibrated growth model with technology shocks could mimic many features of cycles.",works:["Rules Rather than Discretion (1977)","Time to Build and Aggregate Fluctuations (1982)"],influence:"The case for central bank independence; the methods of modern quantitative macro.",critique:"Pure technology shocks struggle to explain recessions; money and demand matter more than RBC allowed.",today:"Time inconsistency explains why central bank independence and fiscal rules matter for Nepal's credibility.",specs:["macro-theory","monetary"]}),e({id:"mundell",name:"Robert Mundell",years:"1932 to 2021",from:"Canada",era:"modern",school:"Open economy macro",nobel:"1999",idea:"The impossible trinity and optimum currency areas: a country cannot have a fixed exchange rate, free capital movement and independent monetary policy all at once.",explain:"The Mundell-Fleming model extends IS-LM to an open economy. Under fixed exchange rates with capital mobility, monetary policy is powerless and fiscal policy strong; under floating rates the reverse. His 1961 paper asked when countries should share a currency: when labour moves freely, shocks are similar and fiscal transfers exist.",works:["A Theory of Optimum Currency Areas (1961)","Capital Mobility and Stabilization Policy under Fixed and Flexible Exchange Rates (1963)"],influence:"Basis of exchange rate regime analysis and of the euro debate.",critique:"Static and simple; modern models add expectations and financial frictions.",today:"Nepal's peg to the Indian rupee is a direct application: open labour border and close trade ties are optimum currency area arguments for the peg.",specs:["intl-finance","monetary"]}),e({id:"akerlof-spence-stiglitz",name:"George Akerlof, Michael Spence and Joseph Stiglitz",years:"born 1940, 1943, 1943",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"Information economics",nobel:"2001",idea:"When one side knows more, markets can unravel (adverse selection), people signal, and lenders ration credit.",explain:"Akerlof's market for lemons: if buyers cannot tell good used cars from bad, they pay an average price, owners of good cars withdraw, and the market can collapse. Spence showed education can signal ability even if it adds nothing to productivity. Stiglitz, with Weiss, showed banks may ration credit rather than raise rates, because higher rates attract riskier borrowers; with Shapiro he explained unemployment as a discipline device (efficiency wages).",works:["The Market for Lemons (Akerlof 1970)","Job Market Signaling (Spence 1973)","Credit Rationing in Markets with Imperfect Information (Stiglitz and Weiss 1981)"],influence:"Information problems are now part of every field, from insurance to development.",critique:"Some markets work better than predicted thanks to warranties, reputation and regulation.",today:"Credit rationing to small farmers and firms in Nepal, and the value of degrees as signals, are information economics questions.",specs:["information-contracts","banking","development"]}),e({id:"sen",name:"Amartya Sen",years:"born 1933",from:"India",era:"modern",school:"Welfare economics and development",nobel:"1998",idea:"Development is the expansion of capabilities, what people are able to do and be; famines are failures of entitlement, not just of food supply.",explain:"Sen showed that the 1943 Bengal famine happened without a large fall in food supply: people starved because their wages and entitlements to food collapsed. He argued that income is only a means; what matters is people's capabilities (health, education, freedom). With Mahbub ul Haq he shaped the Human Development Index. He also contributed to social choice theory and poverty measurement.",works:["Collective Choice and Social Welfare (1970)","Poverty and Famines (1981)","Development as Freedom (1999)"],influence:"Human development reports, multidimensional poverty indices and the focus on gender gaps.",critique:"Capabilities are hard to measure and weight.",today:"Nepal's multidimensional poverty index and human development reports use Sen's approach directly.",topics:["poverty-inequality"],specs:["development","inequality","thought"]}),e({id:"haq",name:"Mahbub ul Haq",years:"1934 to 1998",from:"Pakistan",era:"modern",school:"Human development",idea:"Put people, not GDP, at the centre of development measurement.",explain:"As founder of UNDP's Human Development Report in 1990, Haq created the Human Development Index combining income, education and life expectancy. He wanted a single number simple enough to compete with GDP in public debate.",works:["Reflections on Human Development (1995)","Human Development Report (1990, founding editor)"],influence:"The HDI is published every year for nearly every country.",critique:"The weights in the index are arbitrary.",today:"Nepal's human development reports, with district comparisons, follow his framework.",specs:["development"]}),e({id:"ostrom",name:"Elinor Ostrom",years:"1933 to 2012",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"Institutional (Bloomington)",nobel:"2009 (with Oliver Williamson; the first woman to win)",idea:"Communities can manage common resources well without privatisation or state control, if they design the right rules.",explain:"Hardin's tragedy of the commons predicts that shared resources are overused. Ostrom studied forests, fisheries and irrigation systems around the world and found many communities sustain them for centuries with local rules: clear boundaries, monitoring, graduated sanctions and conflict resolution. She distilled eight design principles.",works:["Governing the Commons (1990)"],influence:"Changed how development agencies approach natural resources.",critique:"The design principles are hard to transplant; success depends on context.",today:"Research by Ostrom and colleagues, including Wai Fung Lam, on irrigation in Nepal found many farmer managed systems performed as well as or better than agency managed ones. Nepal's community forestry is another famous case.",specs:["institutions","environment","agriculture"]}),e({id:"north",name:"Douglass North",years:"1920 to 2015",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"New institutional",nobel:"1993 (with Robert Fogel)",idea:"Institutions, the rules of the game, determine long run economic performance.",explain:"North defined institutions as formal rules (laws, constitutions) and informal constraints (norms) that shape incentives. Secure property rights and credible government reduce transaction costs and encourage investment. With Weingast he argued England's Glorious Revolution of 1688 made government borrowing credible.",works:["Institutions, Institutional Change and Economic Performance (1990)","Constitutions and Commitment (1989, with Weingast)"],influence:"Inspired the institutions and growth literature (Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson).",critique:"Institutions are hard to measure and may themselves be caused by income.",today:"Nepal's transition to federalism in 2015 is a natural experiment in institutional change.",specs:["institutions","econ-history"]}),e({id:"heckman-mcfadden",name:"James Heckman and Daniel McFadden",years:"born 1944; born 1937",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"Microeconometrics",nobel:"2000",idea:"Correct for selection in who we observe (Heckman); model choices among discrete options (McFadden).",explain:"Wages are observed only for people who work, so a wage regression on workers is biased. Heckman's two step method corrects for this selection. McFadden's conditional logit models how people choose among options such as transport modes, and is used for demand estimation everywhere. Heckman later studied early childhood investment.",model:a`P(\text{choose } j) = \frac{e^{V_j}}{\sum_k e^{V_k}}`,works:["Sample Selection Bias as a Specification Error (Heckman 1979)","Conditional Logit Analysis of Qualitative Choice Behavior (McFadden 1974)"],influence:"Selection models and discrete choice are core microeconometrics.",critique:"Heckman's correction depends on functional form unless there is a valid exclusion restriction.",today:"Remittance-receiving households are self-selected, so selection matters for Nepali research.",specs:["applied-econometrics","labour"]}),e({id:"krugman",name:"Paul Krugman",years:"born 1953",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"New trade theory",nobel:"2008",idea:"Increasing returns and love of variety explain trade between similar countries and the concentration of industry in particular places.",explain:"Classic trade theory explained trade from differences between countries. Krugman showed that even identical countries trade because firms gain from scale and consumers like variety. His new economic geography explained why industry clusters in a core while the periphery stays agricultural.",works:["Increasing Returns, Monopolistic Competition, and International Trade (1979)","Increasing Returns and Economic Geography (1991)"],influence:"Modern trade theory and urban economics build on his models.",critique:"Firm heterogeneity (Melitz 2003) and quantitative models later refined the picture.",today:"Why industry concentrates near the Indian border and Kathmandu, not in the hills, is a new economic geography question.",specs:["trade","urban"]}),e({id:"romer",name:"Paul Romer and William Nordhaus",years:"born 1955; born 1941",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"Endogenous growth; climate economics",nobel:"2018",idea:"Ideas drive growth because they can be used by everyone at once (Romer); climate change can be put into economic models to guide carbon pricing (Nordhaus).",explain:"Romer modelled technology as the result of deliberate research: ideas are non-rival, so a bigger stock of ideas makes researchers more productive and growth can continue. Nordhaus built integrated assessment models (DICE) linking emissions, temperature and economic damage, to compute the social cost of carbon.",model:a`\dot A = \delta L_A A \quad \text{(Romer)}`,works:["Endogenous Technological Change (Romer 1990)","An Optimal Transition Path for Controlling Greenhouse Gases (Nordhaus 1992)"],influence:"Growth theory and climate policy analysis.",critique:"Endogenous growth predicts scale effects not seen in data; damage functions in climate models are uncertain.",today:"Nepal is highly exposed to climate change (glacial lakes, floods) while emitting very little: climate economics matters greatly for it.",specs:["growth","climate","innovation"]}),e({id:"kahneman",name:"Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky",years:"1934 to 2024; 1937 to 1996",from:"Israel and United States",era:"evidence",school:"Behavioural",nobel:"2002 (Kahneman, with Vernon Smith; Tversky had died)",idea:"People evaluate outcomes as gains and losses from a reference point, feel losses more than gains, and use mental shortcuts that cause predictable errors.",explain:"Prospect theory replaced expected utility for describing actual choices: value depends on changes, losses loom about twice as large as gains (loss aversion), and people overweight small probabilities. Their work on heuristics showed systematic biases such as anchoring and availability.",model:a`v(x) = \begin{cases} x^{\alpha} & x \ge 0 \\ -\lambda(-x)^{\beta} & x < 0 \end{cases}`,works:["Prospect Theory: An Analysis of Decision under Risk (1979)","Thinking, Fast and Slow (Kahneman 2011)"],influence:"Founded behavioural economics.",critique:"Some findings have not replicated well; context matters.",today:"Loss aversion helps explain why farmers resist switching crops and why savings commitments work.",specs:["behavioural"]}),e({id:"thaler",name:"Richard Thaler",years:"born 1945",from:"United States",era:"evidence",school:"Behavioural",nobel:"2017",idea:"Nudges: small changes in how choices are presented can change behaviour a lot.",explain:"Thaler showed people use mental accounts, lack self-control and stick with defaults. Automatic enrolment in pension plans greatly raises participation because people rarely opt out. Choice architecture can help people without restricting choice.",works:["Nudge (2008, with Cass Sunstein)","Misbehaving (2015)"],influence:"Behavioural insight units in many governments.",critique:"Nudges have modest effects on average; some argue they are paternalistic.",today:"Default enrolment in Nepal's contribution-based social security scheme would be a direct nudge application worth testing.",specs:["behavioural","household-finance"]}),e({id:"deaton",name:"Angus Deaton",years:"born 1945",from:"Scotland and United States",era:"evidence",school:"Applied micro and development",nobel:"2015",idea:"Understand consumption, poverty and welfare from household survey data.",explain:"Deaton (with Muellbauer) built the Almost Ideal Demand System, showed how to measure poverty and welfare from household surveys, and studied consumption smoothing, health and wellbeing. He cautions that randomised trials answer narrow questions and need theory to generalise.",works:["An Almost Ideal Demand System (1980, with Muellbauer)","The Analysis of Household Surveys (1997)","The Great Escape (2013)"],influence:"Household survey methods underpin poverty measurement worldwide.",critique:"Few: his caution on RCTs is itself debated.",today:"The Nepal Living Standards Survey is exactly the kind of data Deaton's methods are built for.",specs:["development","inequality"]}),e({id:"banerjee-duflo-kremer",name:"Abhijit Banerjee, Esther Duflo and Michael Kremer",years:"born 1961, 1972, 1964",from:"India, France, United States",era:"evidence",school:"Experimental development",nobel:"2019",idea:"Break big development questions into smaller ones and test solutions with randomised controlled trials.",explain:"Kremer's school experiments in Kenya (textbooks, deworming) showed rigorous field trials were possible. Banerjee and Duflo, with J-PAL (founded 2003), ran trials on microfinance, teaching at the right level, immunisation incentives and more. The approach changed how aid and governments test policy.",works:["Worms (Miguel and Kremer 2004)","Poor Economics (Banerjee and Duflo 2011)","Good Economics for Hard Times (2019)"],influence:"RCTs are now standard in development economics and policy.",critique:"External validity, ethics and the focus on small questions are debated (Deaton, Pritchett).",today:"Field experiments have been run in Nepal, for example on savings accounts (Prina 2015) and on what potential migrants believe about earnings and risks abroad (Shrestha 2020); this is one of the most accessible routes into a top PhD.",specs:["development","experimental","applied-econometrics"]}),e({id:"card-angrist-imbens",name:"David Card, Joshua Angrist and Guido Imbens",years:"born 1956, 1960, 1963",from:"Canada, United States and Israel, Netherlands",era:"evidence",school:"Credibility revolution",nobel:"2021",idea:"Natural experiments can reveal causal effects; with heterogeneous effects, IV estimates the effect for compliers (LATE).",explain:"Card and Krueger compared fast food employment in New Jersey and Pennsylvania after a minimum wage rise and found no job loss. Card's Mariel boatlift study examined a sudden wave of immigrants. Angrist used the Vietnam draft lottery and quarter of birth as instruments. Angrist and Imbens showed exactly what IV estimates when effects differ across people.",model:a`\hat\beta_{IV} = \frac{E[Y \mid Z = 1] - E[Y \mid Z = 0]}{E[D \mid Z = 1] - E[D \mid Z = 0]} = LATE`,works:["Minimum Wages and Employment (Card and Krueger 1994)","Identification and Estimation of Local Average Treatment Effects (Imbens and Angrist 1994)","Mostly Harmless Econometrics (Angrist and Pischke 2009)"],influence:"Design-based empirical work dominates applied economics today.",critique:"Local effects may not generalise; some natural experiments answer narrow questions.",today:"Nepal has many natural experiments: earthquakes, border closures, federal restructuring, policy cut-offs. Finding one is the start of a strong job market paper.",topics:["endogeneity-iv","did","rdd"],specs:["applied-econometrics","labour"]}),e({id:"bernanke-diamond-dybvig",name:"Ben Bernanke, Douglas Diamond and Philip Dybvig",years:"born 1953, 1953, 1955",from:"United States",era:"evidence",school:"Banking and macro-finance",nobel:"2022",idea:"Banks transform short deposits into long loans, which makes them useful but vulnerable to runs; bank failures deepen recessions.",explain:"Diamond and Dybvig showed that bank runs can be self-fulfilling: if everyone expects others to withdraw, withdrawing is rational. Deposit insurance and a lender of last resort prevent this. Bernanke showed that bank failures in the 1930s deepened the Depression by destroying information about borrowers. As Fed chair he applied these lessons in 2008.",works:["Bank Runs, Deposit Insurance, and Liquidity (Diamond and Dybvig 1983)","Nonmonetary Effects of the Financial Crisis in the Propagation of the Great Depression (Bernanke 1983)"],influence:"Foundations of banking regulation and crisis policy.",critique:"Rescues create moral hazard.",today:"Cooperative failures in Nepal and NRB's role as lender of last resort are direct applications.",specs:["banking","monetary"]}),e({id:"goldin",name:"Claudia Goldin",years:"born 1946",from:"United States",era:"evidence",school:"Economic history and labour",nobel:"2023",idea:"Women's labour market outcomes follow a U shape with development, and the remaining gender pay gap comes mostly from the cost of flexibility within occupations.",explain:"Using two centuries of US data, Goldin showed women's participation fell with industrialisation and rose later with services and education. The modern pay gap opens after the first child, because jobs reward long, inflexible hours (greedy work).",works:["Understanding the Gender Gap (1990)","The Race between Education and Technology (2008, with Katz)","Career and Family (2021)"],influence:"Gender economics and the history of labour markets.",critique:"Mainly US evidence; patterns differ elsewhere.",today:"Women's labour force participation in Nepal is shaped by male migration, care work and agriculture: a promising research field.",specs:["gender","labour","econ-history"]}),e({id:"ajr",name:"Daron Acemoglu, Simon Johnson and James Robinson",years:"born 1967, 1963, 1960",from:"Turkey, England and United States",era:"evidence",school:"Institutions and political economy",nobel:"2024",idea:"Inclusive institutions create prosperity; extractive institutions, often set up by colonisers, keep countries poor.",explain:"In their 2001 paper they used settler mortality as an instrument for colonial institutions: where Europeans could settle, they built institutions protecting property; where disease killed them, they set up extraction. Those institutions persisted and explain much of today's income differences.",works:["The Colonial Origins of Comparative Development (2001)","Why Nations Fail (Acemoglu and Robinson 2012)","Power and Progress (Acemoglu and Johnson 2023)"],influence:"Made institutions central to growth economics.",critique:"The settler mortality data and the instrument's validity have been debated (Albouy).",today:"Nepal was never colonised but had extractive land institutions under the Rana regime; how they shaped today's outcomes is a research question.",specs:["institutions","growth","development"]}),e({id:"aghion-howitt-mokyr",name:"Philippe Aghion, Peter Howitt and Joel Mokyr",years:"born 1956, 1946, 1946",from:"France, Canada, Netherlands and United States",era:"evidence",school:"Innovation-driven growth",nobel:"2025",idea:"Sustained growth comes from innovation that replaces older technologies (creative destruction), and depends on a culture and institutions that welcome useful knowledge.",explain:"Aghion and Howitt built a model of Schumpeter's creative destruction: firms invest in research to win the market, and each innovation makes the previous one obsolete. Competition, patents and finance shape the pace. Mokyr, an economic historian, showed how the Enlightenment's culture of useful knowledge and openness to new ideas made the Industrial Revolution self-sustaining.",model:a`g = \lambda\, n \ln \gamma \quad \text{(growth = research arrival rate times step size)}`,works:["A Model of Growth Through Creative Destruction (Aghion and Howitt 1992)","The Lever of Riches (Mokyr 1990)","A Culture of Growth (Mokyr 2016)"],influence:"The leading framework for growth through innovation and firm dynamics.",critique:"Innovation is hard to measure in poor countries, where adoption matters more than invention.",today:"For Nepal the relevant question is technology adoption and diffusion: why firms do not adopt known better methods.",specs:["innovation","growth","econ-history"]}),e({id:"piketty",name:"Thomas Piketty",years:"born 1971",from:"France",era:"evidence",school:"Inequality and public economics",idea:"When the return on capital exceeds the growth rate (r > g), wealth concentrates over time.",explain:"Using tax records over two centuries, Piketty and co-authors (Saez, Zucman and others) built series of top income and wealth shares. Inequality fell in the 20th century due to wars, depression and progressive taxes, and rose again after 1980. He proposes progressive wealth taxation.",model:a`\beta = \frac{s}{g} \quad \text{(long run capital to income ratio)}`,works:["Capital in the Twenty-First Century (2013 in French, 2014 in English)","Income Inequality in the United States (2003, with Saez)"],influence:"Revived the study of wealth inequality; the World Inequality Database.",critique:"Critics question the r > g mechanism and measurement choices; housing drives much of the rise in capital.",today:"The World Inequality Database has estimates for Nepal; land concentration is the key wealth question there.",specs:["inequality","public-finance"]}),e({id:"minsky",name:"Hyman Minsky",years:"1919 to 1996",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"Post-Keynesian",idea:"Stability is destabilising: calm periods encourage risk taking and debt until a crisis.",explain:"Minsky described borrowers moving from hedge finance (income covers debt payments) to speculative finance (needs refinancing) to Ponzi finance (needs rising asset prices). When prices stop rising, the system collapses: a Minsky moment. He was little cited until 2008 made him famous.",works:["Stabilizing an Unstable Economy (1986)"],influence:"Macroprudential policy and financial cycle research.",critique:"Informal; hard to test and formalise.",today:"Rapid credit growth to land and housing in Nepal, and cooperative failures, fit a Minsky pattern.",specs:["banking","macro-theory"]}),e({id:"simon",name:"Herbert Simon",years:"1916 to 2001",from:"United States",era:"keynes",school:"Behavioural (bounded rationality)",nobel:"1978",idea:"People are boundedly rational: they satisfice rather than optimise because information and computation are costly.",explain:"Simon argued that real decision makers search until they find an option that is good enough. He studied organisations and decision making and was a pioneer of artificial intelligence.",works:["A Behavioral Model of Rational Choice (1955)","Administrative Behavior (1947)"],influence:"Forerunner of behavioural economics and organisational economics.",critique:"Satisficing is hard to model precisely.",today:"Rational inattention models (Sims) formalise his idea; useful for how households process news about prices.",specs:["behavioural"]}),e({id:"sims-sargent",name:"Christopher Sims and Thomas Sargent",years:"born 1942; born 1943",from:"United States",era:"modern",school:"Empirical macro",nobel:"2011",idea:"Use vector autoregressions to measure how shocks spread through the economy; take expectations seriously when estimating policy effects.",explain:"Sims argued that large macro models imposed incredible restrictions and proposed VARs, which let the data show how variables respond to shocks. Sargent studied how expectations and policy regimes interact, for example how hyperinflations ended when fiscal regimes changed.",model:a`y_t = c + A_1 y_{t-1} + \dots + A_p y_{t-p} + \varepsilon_t`,works:["Macroeconomics and Reality (Sims 1980)","The Ends of Four Big Inflations (Sargent 1982)"],influence:"VARs and local projections are standard tools at every central bank.",critique:"Identifying structural shocks requires assumptions that are often debated.",today:"A VAR of Nepal's inflation with Indian inflation, remittances and the policy rate is a feasible first research project.",topics:["time-series-arma"],specs:["time-series","monetary"]}),e({id:"tirole",name:"Jean Tirole",years:"born 1953",from:"France",era:"evidence",school:"Industrial organisation",nobel:"2014",idea:"How to regulate industries with a few powerful firms, including platforms that serve two sides of a market.",explain:"Tirole, with Laffont, built a theory of regulation under asymmetric information. With Rochet he analysed two-sided platforms (card networks, app stores) where prices on one side affect demand on the other.",works:["The Theory of Industrial Organization (1988)","A Theory of Incentives in Procurement and Regulation (1993, with Laffont)","Platform Competition in Two-Sided Markets (2003, with Rochet)"],influence:"Competition policy and digital platform regulation.",critique:"Models are complex and conclusions depend on details.",today:"Regulating digital wallets, telecoms and electricity in Nepal raises Tirole's questions.",specs:["io","digital"]}),e({id:"roth-shapley",name:"Alvin Roth and Lloyd Shapley",years:"born 1951; 1923 to 2016",from:"United States",era:"evidence",school:"Market design",nobel:"2012",idea:"Stable matching: design matching markets so no pair would rather match with each other than with their assigned partners.",explain:"Gale and Shapley (1962) gave the deferred acceptance algorithm for stable matching. Roth used it to redesign the US medical residency match, school choice in New York and Boston, and kidney exchanges.",works:["College Admissions and the Stability of Marriage (Gale and Shapley 1962)","Who Gets What and Why (Roth 2015)"],influence:"Market design is now a practical field.",critique:"Real markets have complications (couples, strategic behaviour).",today:"Allocating seats in Nepal's public medical colleges or schools could use deferred acceptance.",specs:["market-design"]})],o=[{id:"classical",name:"Classical",core:"Markets coordinate through competition; growth comes from capital accumulation and specialisation; supply creates its own demand.",people:"Smith, Ricardo, Malthus, Mill",right:"Specialisation, trade and incentives.",weak:"Little room for persistent unemployment.",now:"Lives on in growth and trade theory."},{id:"marxian",name:"Marxian",core:"Class conflict and capital accumulation drive history; profit comes from surplus value.",people:"Marx, Engels, Kalecki",right:"Attention to distribution, power and crises.",weak:"Labour theory of value; failed predictions.",now:"Influential in political economy and in Nepali politics."},{id:"neoclassical",name:"Neoclassical",core:"Individuals optimise at the margin; prices clear markets; equilibrium and efficiency.",people:"Marshall, Walras, Pareto, Samuelson, Arrow",right:"A rigorous common language.",weak:"Strong rationality and complete market assumptions.",now:"The core of every PhD first year."},{id:"keynesian",name:"Keynesian and New Keynesian",core:"Demand determines output in the short run; prices and wages are sticky, so policy can stabilise.",people:"Keynes, Hicks, Samuelson; New Keynesians Mankiw, Woodford, Gali",right:"Recessions are real and policy can help.",weak:"Early versions ignored expectations.",now:"New Keynesian models are standard at central banks."},{id:"monetarist",name:"Monetarist",core:"Money growth drives inflation; markets are fairly stable; prefer rules to discretion.",people:"Friedman, Schwartz",right:"Inflation is ultimately monetary; the natural rate.",weak:"Money targeting failed when velocity changed.",now:"Absorbed into inflation targeting."},{id:"new-classical",name:"New classical and real business cycle",core:"Rational expectations; models built on optimising agents; cycles as efficient responses to shocks.",people:"Lucas, Sargent, Kydland, Prescott",right:"Expectations and the Lucas critique.",weak:"Pure RBC understates demand and money.",now:"Its methods (DSGE) are universal."},{id:"austrian",name:"Austrian",core:"Knowledge is dispersed; markets are a discovery process; skeptical of central planning and credit booms.",people:"Menger, Mises, Hayek, Schumpeter",right:"Information and entrepreneurship.",weak:"Rejects formal and empirical methods.",now:"Influence through information economics and innovation research."},{id:"institutional",name:"Institutional and new institutional",core:"Rules, norms and transaction costs shape outcomes.",people:"Veblen, Commons, Coase, North, Williamson, Ostrom, Acemoglu",right:"Why the same policy works in one place and fails in another.",weak:"Hard to measure institutions.",now:"Central in development and political economy."},{id:"behavioural",name:"Behavioural",core:"People have limited attention, present bias and social preferences; these change predictions.",people:"Simon, Kahneman, Tversky, Thaler, Laibson",right:"Predictable departures from rationality.",weak:"Many biases, no unified theory; replication concerns.",now:"Mainstream in micro and policy."},{id:"structuralist",name:"Development and structuralist",core:"Poor economies have different structures (dual sectors, commodity dependence) needing active policy.",people:"Lewis, Prebisch, Myrdal, Hirschman, Sen",right:"Structural change matters.",weak:"Import substitution often failed.",now:"Revived in industrial policy debates."},{id:"post-keynesian",name:"Post-Keynesian",core:"Fundamental uncertainty, endogenous money and financial instability.",people:"Joan Robinson, Kalecki, Minsky",right:"Finance can destabilise economies.",weak:"Less formal; smaller research community.",now:"Influences macro-financial thinking after 2008."}];Object.fromEntries(n.map(i=>[i.id,i]));export{t as E,o as S,n as T};
